/**
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  GENE KEYS — EVOLUTION  (position-specific readings)
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *  The point opposite the Sun: the friction position. What life keeps
 *  handing you back until you learn it, most often through other people and
 *  close relationships — the recurring pattern rather than the easy
 *  expression of the wiring.
 *
 *  Five-field psychological/behavioral entries per docs/GOLDEN-STANDARD-PSYCH.md:
 *  wiring, root, defaultMode, blindSpot, underusedStrength. Second person,
 *  present tense, no hedging, no spiritual-arc vocabulary. Every entry is
 *  written fresh for the recurring-life-lesson lens and shares no phrasing
 *  with the universal reading in js/gene-keys-content.js or with
 *  js/gk-lifeswork.js.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 */

DGKRoles.register('evolution', {
  "1": {
    "wiring": "You bring freshness back into relationships that have quietly gone flat, and the people closest to you feel the air change within a few minutes of your arrival. You begin again after a long stall without bitterness and without a speech. You give the people who love you permission to stop performing an old, agreed version of themselves.",
    "root": "Sameness in a close bond got read early on as a warning sign rather than as safety, so staying fresh with someone became the thing you actually trusted more than staying steady. Comfort started to look suspiciously like stagnation. That association is old and still runs underneath most of your closest relationships.",
    "defaultMode": "The recurring pattern is that something you were once alive inside goes stale, and instead of naming it out loud you drift, letting entropy do the leaving for you. Under pressure you mistake ordinary boredom for proof the other person has failed you. Conflict finds you flat — you go quiet, agree to whatever ends the discussion fastest, and privately withdraw the exact part of you that made the connection worth having.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that you're the one who let the connection go stale by disengaging quietly, not the other person who simply kept showing up the same way. Others notice your withdrawal well before you'd call it that yourself. What feels like the relationship failing to you often looks, from outside, like you deciding it had.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to bring real freshness back into an old bond is a rare relational skill most people lose within a few years of any commitment. Naming the staleness out loud the moment you notice it, instead of quietly scanning for an exit, gives the relationship the same chance to renew that a stranger's novelty would have given it. The freshness was never actually gone; it just needed to be spoken instead of withheld."
  },
  "2": {
    "wiring": "You find your bearings again after being thrown, and the people around you borrow that steadiness long before they notice they're doing it. You ask which direction actually matters rather than demanding a full map first. You bring a lost conversation back to its real subject, and everyone present relaxes into finding themselves on the same side of the table again.",
    "root": "Belonging felt conditional on being useful or agreeable early on, so you learned to locate yourself by other people's coordinates rather than trust your own sense of direction. Home started to feel like somewhere else, held by somebody else. That displacement is old and predates most of the relationships you now bring it into.",
    "defaultMode": "You arrive somewhere that should fit you perfectly, and within months you feel subtly out of place without being able to say why. When closeness deepens you lose your own direction inside it, taking on their preferences and their pace until you resent a life you agreed to. In conflict you go abstract and unreachable, and the other person experiences your disorientation as a coldness they somehow caused.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that the displacement is coming from inside you rather than from wherever you currently are. Others notice you drifting before you'd admit you'd started. What feels like the wrong place to you sometimes is actually just you, unmoored from your own direction again.",
    "underusedStrength": "Your instinct for what direction actually matters is a real relational advantage most people never develop, since they mistake motion for progress. Naming your own bearing out loud early in a relationship, before you've absorbed someone else's entirely, keeps the connection oriented by two directions instead of one borrowed one. The compass was never broken; it just needed checking before it got overridden."
  },
  "3": {
    "wiring": "You meet disorder without needing it tidied or explained first, and the people close to you calm down simply because you aren't panicking alongside them. You turn a mess into a first attempt faster than almost anyone else in the room. You let the people you love stay unfinished for as long as they need.",
    "root": "Fixed plans failed you early on in a way that made improvising feel like the only reliable skill, and that instability got carried straight into how you approach closeness. Commitment to one shape never got the same reinforcement that adapting fast did. The pattern is old and mostly automatic now.",
    "defaultMode": "Everything starts at once, nothing settles, and the people nearest you feel that churn as an instability they can't plan around. Early in intimacy you burn hot and inventive, then the confusion arrives and you read it as a sign the whole thing was wrong. When pressed for a decision you generate three further options and call it thinking, while the person waiting hears it as plain avoidance.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how many promising closenesses you've abandoned exactly where they stopped being play and became work. Others notice the pattern of beginnings that never quite become established before you'd name it yourself. What feels like the relationship losing its spark to you sometimes is just the ordinary maintenance stage you haven't learned to stay through yet.",
    "underusedStrength": "Tolerance for relational disorder is a genuine advantage most people never develop, since they need certainty before they can commit at all. Staying inside one relationship's unglamorous middle, using the same nerve you already bring to chaotic beginnings, is the missing piece rather than a different skill. What survives past the mess becomes closeness that most people never reach."
  },
  "4": {
    "wiring": "You hold your conclusions loosely enough that a person changes your mind mid-sentence, and they feel that possibility in you before you've said a word. You reach understanding by staying inside a question far longer than is comfortable. You let others be wrong in front of you without losing any warmth.",
    "root": "An unanswered question felt like danger early on, so producing a fast, confident conclusion became a way to feel safe rather than actually a way to be right. That anxiety about not-knowing is old and still drives more of your certainty than you'd guess. It formed before you had the patience to sit in it that you have now.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone's reasoning arrives half-formed and something in you tightens well before they've finished the sentence. You answer the question they didn't ask, and the correction lands as contempt no matter how carefully you phrase it. In close relationships you build a private case file, and by the time you raise it you're delivering a verdict, not discussing an issue.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when you've built a case against someone in your head that they never had a chance to answer to directly. Others notice the accumulated verdict arriving all at once before you'd realize you'd been compiling it. What feels like patience to you while you gather evidence sometimes reads to them as quiet, building judgment.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine tolerance for an unresolved question is a rare relational skill most people abandon at the first plausible answer. Saying what's bothering you the first time it occurs, rather than filing it toward a later verdict, keeps the relationship in real dialogue instead of a one-sided trial. The patience was never wrong; keeping it private for too long is what turns it into judgment."
  },
  "5": {
    "wiring": "You keep your own rhythm when other people are rushing you, and the ones who trust you have learned your timing is reliably sound. You practise patience as an active skill rather than resignation. You let relationships ripen on their own schedule.",
    "root": "Rushing produced worse outcomes early on than waiting did, and that lesson generalized into a working theory that anything allowed to ripen on its own would turn out better than anything forced. Steadiness became identity rather than just a preference. That belief is old and still runs your closest relationships.",
    "defaultMode": "Other people's pace feels too slow, too vague, too late, and your body tightens long before your manners catch up. You push a decision that wasn't ready, and the person agrees only to end the pressure, which quietly costs you their honesty. In intimacy you check for progress — are we further along than last month — and turn a living thing into a schedule it can fail.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that your own pushing is what's producing the very unripeness you're impatient about. Others notice the pressure building before you'd admit you were the one applying it. What feels like waiting to you sometimes is actually forcing dressed up as patience.",
    "underusedStrength": "Real patience — the kind that lets something ripen without checking its progress constantly — is a rare relational skill most people can't sustain past the first uncomfortable delay. Naming your impatience out loud instead of acting on it silently lets the other person know the pressure is coming from you, not from the pace of the relationship itself. What grows slowly with you, left actually unrushed, outlasts what grows fast everywhere else."
  },
  "6": {
    "wiring": "You stay in the room when a conversation turns hot, and that alone changes what's possible between two people who were both about to harden against each other. You practise honesty with good timing rather than smoothing over. You repair after a rupture, which is the one skill most relationships lack.",
    "root": "Peace and honesty felt like they couldn't occupy the same conversation early on, so you learned to pick one, usually peace, at the cost of the other. That trade-off is old and still shapes how you handle friction now. It formed before you'd tested whether both could actually coexist.",
    "defaultMode": "Closeness reaches a certain depth and then a boundary dispute erupts over something small enough to be genuinely embarrassing. You either detonate to get the truth onto the table or swallow it and go cold, and both endings teach the other person to tread carefully. In arguments you defend the version of events that makes you look reasonable, which wins you the hour and costs you the intimacy.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that avoiding a small honest friction now is what's building the larger rupture you'll eventually have instead. Others notice you smoothing something over faster than the situation calls for before you'd admit you'd done it. What you call keeping the peace sometimes reads to them as avoiding the actual issue.",
    "underusedStrength": "Staying in the room through real friction is a genuine relational skill most people never develop, since they either flee or escalate. Naming the actual disagreement plainly, the first time it appears, rather than after it's compounded, resolves things faster than years of smoothing ever manages. The instinct for peace was never wrong; it just needed honesty running alongside it instead of instead of it."
  },
  "7": {
    "wiring": "You offer guidance that people actually take, because you give it as a direction rather than an instruction and leave their own authority intact. You read where a group is heading before it knows itself. You draw others into a shared aim, and they work better alongside you than they expected to.",
    "root": "Being right on your own, without the group's agreement, felt hollow early on, so leading came to mean bringing others along rather than simply being correct in isolation. That need for shared direction is old. It formed before you'd learned the difference between leading a group and leading alone.",
    "defaultMode": "A group you care about splits down the middle, and you feel pulled to lead one half against the other. You know where things should go, and when people choose differently you privately downgrade them, which they sense long before you say it. In close relationships you slip into steering — deciding what's best for someone who didn't ask.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how often you've quietly stopped consulting the very people you claim to be leading. Others notice the shift from guidance to steering before you'd name it yourself. What feels like clarity to you sometimes reads to them as being managed rather than accompanied.",
    "underusedStrength": "Guidance offered without needing credit for it is genuinely rare and it's why people trust your direction more than most. Checking your read with the group before acting on it, instead of after, keeps the leadership actually shared instead of just quietly assumed. The instinct for direction was never the problem; skipping the consultation was."
  },
  "8": {
    "wiring": "You carry a style that is unmistakably yours, and the people around you loosen their own edges because you've made being distinct look survivable. You notice at once when something has been done merely adequately. You bring your whole particular self into a relationship.",
    "root": "Standing out at some point cost you something — attention you didn't want, comparison, friction — and blending into a group felt safer than staying visibly distinct. That safety calculation is old and still runs underneath how you show up in close relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether being fully yourself was actually survivable.",
    "defaultMode": "You dim your own colour to fit into a group, then resent that group for the beige version of you it received. When someone close to you outshines you, comparison arrives fast and hard and you either compete quietly or dismiss what they made. You hold back your real contribution until it's flawless, and the moment passes with the room never having met you at all.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that the dimming is a choice you're making, not something being done to you by the group. Others notice you disappearing into agreeableness before you'd call it that yourself. What feels like fitting in to you sometimes reads to them as you simply not being there.",
    "underusedStrength": "A recognizable, undimmed self is a real relational asset — it's what makes people love the specific person rather than a generally likable one. Bringing your actual, particular reaction into a room once, without editing it first, tests whether the connection was ever really about the general-purpose version you'd been offering. The distinctness was never the risk; hiding it was."
  },
  "9": {
    "wiring": "You return to the small thing daily long after the excitement has gone, and that determination is why the people close to you trust your promises without checking. You break a mountain into a week of unremarkable actions. You stay with people through their slow stretches.",
    "root": "Small, sustained effort got more reliable reward early on than big dramatic gestures did, and that shaped which kind of care you learned to trust — the accumulating kind rather than the grand one. Big gestures often went unnoticed in a way steady ones didn't. That preference is old.",
    "defaultMode": "What matters most to you looks trivial to others, and their shrug lands as a verdict on your whole effort. You wait for a big enough reason before starting, and the waiting hardens into an inertia you then read as a character flaw. In close relationships you focus so tightly on one small detail that the person in front of you starts to feel like an interruption.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much your steady, small-scale care goes unnoticed by you as care, because it doesn't feel dramatic enough to count. Others notice the ordinary consistency long before you'd credit yourself for it. What you dismiss as merely showing up is, to them, the entire reason they trust you.",
    "underusedStrength": "Steady, ordinary presence across long stretches is a real relational advantage most people lack entirely — they run hot and quit while you run steady and stay. Naming your small daily acts of staying as the actual love, rather than waiting for a grand gesture to prove it, lets the relationship register what's already true. Nothing here needs to be bigger to count."
  },
  "10": {
    "wiring": "You relax into being yourself in company, and that naturalness gives everyone nearby permission to drop the version of themselves they walked in wearing. You meet people without a performance running underneath. You love people as they actually are rather than as they present.",
    "root": "Being acceptable felt conditional early on — a somewhat improved, carefully edited version of you got the approval, not the plain one — so a habit of monitoring your own presentation got built before you noticed you were doing it. That conditional self-acceptance is old. It runs underneath moments that otherwise feel completely natural.",
    "defaultMode": "You replay your own behaviour after every meaningful encounter, hunting for the exact moment you were too much. Under strain your attention collapses inward and the person sitting in front of you becomes an audience rather than company. In conflict you defend your self-image instead of addressing what actually happened.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of your closeness with someone is spent managing their impression of you rather than actually being with them. Others sense they're being asked to reassure you before you'd admit that's what's happening. What feels like self-awareness to you sometimes reads to them as needing constant management.",
    "underusedStrength": "Unmanufactured presence is genuinely rare, and it's exactly what makes people drop their own guard around you. Staying with the plain, unedited version of an interaction, even when your instinct says to review and correct it afterward, gives you access to the ease people already sense in you. Naturalness was never something to protect through vigilance; the vigilance is what interrupts it."
  },
  "11": {
    "wiring": "You lift the people around you with an idealism they'd long ago privately given up on, and they act on it far more than they'll admit. You see what a person could become and speak directly to that version of them. You bring real colour and fresh possibility into ordinary rooms.",
    "root": "Imagining a better version of a person or situation felt safer early on than facing the plain, unimproved reality in front of you, so the habit of picturing potential became a refuge as much as a gift. That habit is old and still colours how you meet people now. It formed before you'd tested whether the ordinary version was actually enough.",
    "defaultMode": "You build a vivid picture of how things will be, and reality shows up smaller, slower, and plainer than you drew it. When a person fails your ideal you go quietly obscure, retreating into your own imagination where the better version of them still lives. In intimacy you love the potential you see rather than the human actually present.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when you've stopped meeting the actual person and started measuring them against a version that doesn't exist. Others feel the comparison before you'd admit you were making one. What feels like belief in someone to you sometimes reads to them as never quite being enough as they are.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to see real potential in people is a genuine relational gift most people lose after being disappointed a few times. Noticing and naming one plain, ordinary thing about the person in front of you, without reaching for the improved version, lets the actual relationship register instead of the imagined one. The vision was never the problem; requiring the present moment to match it was."
  },
  "12": {
    "wiring": "You sort what's true from what merely sounds good in the moment, and people take your yes and your no equally seriously because of it. You know when to speak and when your silence carries more weight. You say the clean, undecorated sentence a difficult moment needs.",
    "root": "Being seen accurately felt riskier early on than being seen favorably, so managing your image became a habit that ran alongside your actual honesty rather than in service of it. That split is old and still shapes how much of yourself you let show. It formed before you'd tested what happened when the unpolished version got seen.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone misreads you, and image management quietly takes over the whole relationship from there. You withhold speech at the very moment it was needed, then call the withholding discernment when it was mostly self-protection. In conflict your words sharpen into something exact and cutting, and the accuracy of it makes the wound much harder to forgive.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when your careful selectivity about what to say has become protecting your image rather than protecting the truth. Others notice you holding back at exactly the vulnerable moments before you'd call it that yourself. What feels like discretion to you sometimes reads to them as never quite being reachable.",
    "underusedStrength": "The ability to say the one clean sentence a moment actually needs is a rare relational skill most people bury under ten unnecessary ones. Letting one unpolished, unfiltered thing through occasionally, before you've decided how it will land, gives people access to the parts of you your discernment usually screens out. The filter was never wrong; running everything through it left nothing spontaneous."
  },
  "13": {
    "wiring": "You hear what people are not saying, and your discernment about who to trust with what has saved you and others from real harm more than once. You listen without quietly collecting ammunition for later. You gather the true story from many voices.",
    "root": "Being useful to other people's pain felt like the safest way to matter early on, so listening became both a genuine gift and a strategy for being valued. That equation is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether you'd be wanted without it.",
    "defaultMode": "You become the person everyone confides in, and then the unpaid keeper of tensions you never agreed to carry. Listening turns into absorbing, and you take on grief and grievance until you can no longer locate your own feeling in the pile. In close relationships you hear the criticism inside a neutral remark and withdraw, leaving the other person confused about what they'd done.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how rarely you offer your own story compared to how consistently you receive everyone else's. Others don't ask about your inner life because you rarely open a door to it. What feels like being steady and available to you sometimes costs you being actually known.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine listening is in chronic short supply, and turning it on yourself — actually voicing your own weather to someone instead of only receiving theirs — produces relief you rarely let yourself feel. Asking directly for the kind of attention you give away for free is not an imposition; it's a fair trade almost nobody offers you first. The accuracy you bring to other people's inner lives works on your own, once you turn it that direction."
  },
  "14": {
    "wiring": "You deliver competence that people build on, and that reliability quietly holds up more of your relationships than anyone has thought to thank you for. You work from a steady inner fire rather than borrowed motivation. You share generously, and abundance around you is a practical fact rather than a mood.",
    "root": "A good-enough arrangement felt safer to commit to early on than one built around what you actually loved, especially if love and stability seemed to conflict. Practicality got framed as the responsible choice in close relationships too. That compromise was made before you were old enough to weigh it properly.",
    "defaultMode": "You accept the arrangement that works well enough for now, and whole years pass inside a good-enough version of your own life. When someone close asks for more of you, you hand them the labour instead of the presence, then wonder why they still feel unmet. In conflict you reach straight for the practical fix, and the person in front of you wanted to be heard rather than solved.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much genuine wanting you've substituted with competent management in your closest relationships. Others notice they're getting your effort but not quite your presence before you'd admit the difference. What reads as generosity to you sometimes reads to them as being handled rather than truly met.",
    "underusedStrength": "Energy that follows genuine desire is a resource most people spend a lifetime trying to manufacture through discipline instead. Naming what you actually want from a relationship, out loud, rather than defaulting to what's sensible, reveals fast whether the flatness is about the relationship or about the compromise underneath it. Most people never run this experiment long enough to find out."
  },
  "15": {
    "wiring": "You draw people toward you without visible effort, and your magnetism works because you're genuinely interested in them rather than trying to be interesting. You move in your own rhythm of surges and lulls. You make room for the odd, the intense, and the unusual.",
    "root": "Being wanted at full brightness felt like the only version of you that was acceptable early on, so the quiet, low-tide version got hidden rather than shown. That belief is old and still shapes what you let people see. It formed before you'd tested whether the whole range was actually welcome.",
    "defaultMode": "Your natural extremes unsettle people who want you predictable, and you start flattening yourself into a steadier, duller fake. In the lull you feel unlovable, and you either disappear entirely for weeks or manufacture some drama to prove you're still alive. When a relationship becomes routine you read that ordinariness as evidence it's quietly dying.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that hiding your low tide is what's actually preventing anyone from meeting the whole of you. Others sense the edited version before you'd admit you were editing. What feels like protecting the relationship to you sometimes reads to them as never quite being let in.",
    "underusedStrength": "The range you carry — real intensity and real stillness both — is a genuine relational asset most people find hard to access in themselves at all. Letting someone see the low-tide version deliberately, instead of waiting to be caught in it, changes what the relationship is actually built on. The pull you generate at full brightness was never the whole offer; the quiet version deserves the same welcome."
  },
  "16": {
    "wiring": "You adapt across skills, roles, and situations with a versatility most people mistake for luck. You bring an enthusiasm that is genuinely contagious, and people beside you attempt things they'd quietly ruled out. You commit to what you love long past the point of novelty.",
    "root": "Depth in one thing felt riskier early on than breadth across several, since depth exposes a ceiling that breadth conveniently avoids testing. Range became both a strength and a way to dodge being fully evaluated. That trade-off is old and still shapes how deep you let relationships go.",
    "defaultMode": "You learn something up to the interesting stage, then indifference arrives exactly as it starts getting difficult. Enthusiasm sweeps you into commitments to people, and the withdrawal that follows lands on them as betrayal rather than fatigue. In relationships you skim — charming, involved, never quite arrived — and the other person can't name what's missing, only that something is.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how often you exit right when a relationship would require you to be tested rather than merely enjoyed. Others notice the pattern of warm involvement followed by quiet retreat before you'd name it yourself. What feels like losing interest to you sometimes reads to them as never having been fully in.",
    "underusedStrength": "The ability to bring genuine range and enthusiasm into a relationship is a real gift most people never develop, staying instead in one register their whole life. Committing to a single relationship past the point where the novelty runs out — the same discipline you'd apply to a skill you truly wanted to master — converts your natural range into a depth specialists at commitment can't match. Most people with your versatility never test what it produces given real duration."
  },
  "17": {
    "wiring": "You see where a situation is heading long before the people around you do, and that early sight is the source of friction in your closest relationships. You listen for the fear underneath someone's position instead of arguing with the words on the surface. You state a clear view and still leave the door wide open.",
    "root": "Being wrong once, in front of someone whose opinion mattered, felt like it would cost you your standing with them permanently, so certainty became a way to avoid ever being caught out again. That fear is old and still runs beneath how tightly you hold a position. It formed before you'd learned that being wrong occasionally didn't actually cost what you feared.",
    "defaultMode": "Your view hardens into the answer before anyone else in the room has finished their sentence. In a disagreement you defend the position itself rather than the live seeing that originally produced it. You file people into fixed categories and quietly stop looking, so the ones closest to you get managed rather than genuinely known.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when you've stopped actually looking at someone and started running them through an old, filed conclusion instead. Others notice being categorized before you'd admit you'd stopped updating the file. What reads to you as knowing someone well sometimes reads to them as being permanently pre-judged.",
    "underusedStrength": "Seeing patterns before they're visible to anyone else is a genuinely rare relational gift, and it lands best when offered as a live observation rather than a fixed verdict. Re-opening one filed conclusion about someone you love, on purpose, and actually looking again, keeps the relationship current instead of running on an old read. The sight was never inaccurate; treating it as permanent was the actual error."
  },
  "18": {
    "wiring": "You uphold a standard the people around you quietly rely on, and relationships keep testing whether you can name it without wounding anyone. You spot the flaw in an arrangement quickly and raise it as care rather than a verdict. You repair things other people abandon.",
    "root": "Correction that wasn't handled gently early on left you needing to know exactly how it should be done — precise but not humiliating. Love, somewhere in there, started to feel like it arrives only once everything has been corrected. That belief is old and still colours how easily you can simply enjoy what's already there.",
    "defaultMode": "Your eye for the flaw turns on the person in front of you instead of the pattern. Judgement feels like precision from the inside, and in close quarters it lands on the other person as a running audit they never agreed to. You turn it inward as well, replaying your own faults until nothing you did that day counts for anything.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how constant, even fair, correction reads to the people receiving it as never quite being enough. Others notice they've stopped relaxing fully around you before you'd admit you'd stopped offering the chance to. What feels like helpfulness to you sometimes feels like grading to them.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to spot what's actually broken, precisely and without cruelty, is rare and valuable in any relationship, most of which either avoid the problem or handle its naming badly. Deciding which flaws are actually worth naming — and letting the rest go unmentioned — makes the corrections you do offer land as care instead of audit. The eye was never the problem; the filter around when to use it is."
  },
  "19": {
    "wiring": "You feel what a room needs before anybody in it says a word, and closeness is exactly where that reading gets its hardest workout. You ask plainly for what you want now, which people who feel less than you have never had to learn. You give people near you permission to be human out loud, messily and unfinished.",
    "root": "Meeting other people's needs accurately got you closer, safer, or more valued early on than asserting your own needs did. Reading the room became a survival skill before it became a social gift. The habit of scanning for what others require, before checking your own, is old.",
    "defaultMode": "Your antenna picks up a need and your entire schedule quietly bends itself around it. Co-dependence starts as generosity, then hardens into a deal nobody spoke aloud, where you supply the care and wait to be chosen for having supplied it. You go touchy and withdrawn when the return doesn't come, and the other person has no idea what actually changed between you.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how rarely you report your own needs compared to how consistently you track everyone else's. Others notice you monitoring the emotional weather of a room while your own goes unmentioned. People close to you sometimes only find out something was wrong for you well after the fact.",
    "underusedStrength": "The bandwidth you have for reading other people is a genuine asset, and turning even a fraction of it toward yourself — noticing and naming a need before it becomes urgent — changes the whole dynamic of your closest relationships. Stating a specific, small need directly, without waiting to be asked, is a skill you already have in reverse and simply haven't pointed at yourself. Most people never develop this level of attunement to use on anyone, including themselves."
  },
  "20": {
    "wiring": "You reach people exactly where they are, and life keeps introducing you to those who want something deeper than you first offered. You stay fully present in a hard exchange long after others have started reaching for their phones. You speak straight from the moment without rehearsing.",
    "root": "Being fully seen felt like a risk early on, so an easy, engaging surface became the version of you that got offered first, kept in reserve for whoever proved they'd stay. That habit of skimming your own depth before offering it is old. It formed before you'd tested whether the unedited version would actually be welcomed.",
    "defaultMode": "You skim a conversation so that no one gets far enough in to disappoint you later. You fill the silences with activity and charm, then wonder why the people around you still feel like acquaintances after ten years of friendship. Beneath the ease sits a quiet suspicion that whoever meets the unedited version of you will politely move on.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of your warmth is genuinely offered but strategically shallow, protecting a deeper layer that rarely gets tested. Others sense the smoothness before you'd admit it was a form of distance. What feels like being easy to be around to you sometimes reads to them as never quite getting close.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine, unhurried presence is rare enough that most people can name only a handful of interactions in their life where they actually got it from someone. Staying in one moment past the point where you'd normally move the conversation along gives someone real access to the depth your charm usually screens. This costs nothing and the people closest to you would notice the difference immediately."
  },
  "21": {
    "wiring": "You shoulder responsibility that other people quietly put down, and the recurring test of your life is whether you hold it without gripping it. You defend the people in your care fiercely, without needing to run their lives for them. You've learned to be disliked for a decision rather than fold.",
    "root": "If you weren't in charge of a situation early on, it tended to go wrong, and that evidence wired responsibility to safety in a way that's outlasted most of the original circumstances. Control became the thing that kept people from getting hurt. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships.",
    "defaultMode": "Anyone who won't do it your way becomes the friction. Control looks like competence from inside your own head, and it lands on a partner as a hand permanently on the wheel. You take over tasks that were never yours to begin with, then feel unsupported by people you never actually allowed to contribute.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much your grip is costing you in terms of what capable people around you stop offering. Others notice they've quietly stopped volunteering initiative around you before you notice they've stopped. What you experience as diligence sometimes reads to them as being managed.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine ownership — the kind that actually protects something rather than just controlling it — is rare, and it's the reason people trust you with things they wouldn't trust to anyone else. Deliberately handing over one thing you currently control, without checking on it, tests whether your grip is protecting the outcome or just protecting your own sense of security. What flourishes without your hand on it tells you something useful either way."
  },
  "22": {
    "wiring": "You steady other people through emotional weather that would knock most of them sideways, and intimacy is precisely where that skill was forged. You forgive without pretending nothing happened, which is far harder than either forgetting entirely or holding on. You bring dignity back into rooms where it had gone missing.",
    "root": "Emotional composure got a stronger, more reliably positive response early on than visible distress did, so managing your own weight became a skill you built early and well. Being the steady one in a volatile situation kept things from getting worse. That protective habit is old.",
    "defaultMode": "A feeling grows larger than the room around you can hold, and the charm curdles — you either flood the situation with emotion or freeze into a cold politeness that punishes the other person without a single word. Shame follows fast, and you replay the entire scene for days while telling everyone involved you're perfectly fine.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see, from inside the composed periods, how much pressure is actually accumulating underneath. Others sometimes see the storm building before you're willing to name it as one. What looks like sudden loss of control to you often looked, from outside, like it had been coming for a while.",
    "underusedStrength": "Full emotional feeling, expressed at the time rather than suppressed and stored, prevents the eventual overflow you're managing against. Naming a feeling out loud — angry, grieving, humiliated — the moment it arrives, rather than composing yourself first, actually produces less disruption than the storm that builds from suppression. Most people never learn this because they've been taught composure means containment."
  },
  "23": {
    "wiring": "You cut through tangle to the one thing that actually matters, and relationships keep asking you to say it kindly and at the right moment. You wait until somebody is genuinely ready to hear you. You name the thing plainly while everyone else in the room is still circling it.",
    "root": "Being this direct once felt like it made you strange to people, so you learned to either armour the words in extra explanation or swallow them whole rather than risk delivering them plainly. That fear is old and still shapes how much of your clarity you actually let out. It formed before you'd tested whether directness was actually welcome.",
    "defaultMode": "The clarity arrives faster than anybody around you is able to absorb it. You overexplain, hedge, and bury a simple point under qualifications until nobody is able to object to it. In conflict you either land a blunt truth at the worst possible moment or say nothing for months and quietly call that patience.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when your explanation has stopped clarifying and started protecting you from the exposure of a plain statement. Others notice the burying before you'd call it that yourself. What feels like being thorough to you sometimes reads to them as evasive or nervous.",
    "underusedStrength": "The ability to cut to the one thing that matters is genuinely rare, and most people who have it undercut it by over-explaining anyway out of nerves. Trusting a single plain sentence to do the work, without a defensive layer of qualification around it, produces exactly the relief you're actually capable of giving. The skill is intact; the habit of armoring it is the only thing standing between you and using it well."
  },
  "24": {
    "wiring": "You build fresh answers out of thoughts you've already circled a thousand times, and closeness keeps interrupting that loop at exactly the right point. You let a conversation rest overnight instead of forcing a resolution out of everyone while tired and defensive. You return to a question until it finally opens.",
    "root": "The empty moment felt intolerable early on, so filling it — with thinking, with reassurance-seeking, with contact you didn't really want — became the reflex rather than sitting inside the gap. That fear of empty space is old and still drives more of your relational habits than the actual thoughts do. It formed before you'd tested whether the gap itself was survivable.",
    "defaultMode": "You keep returning to a thought or a habit long past any usefulness it once had — the same worry, the same replayed conversation, the same reassurance requested from a partner who already gave it twice. You disappear inside your own head halfway through a conversation, and the person across from you experiences it plainly as absence.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see, while inside a loop, that you're avoiding a gap rather than actually thinking something through. Others notice you circling the same worry before you'd call it a loop yourself. What feels like processing to you sometimes looks, from outside, like being stuck.",
    "underusedStrength": "Tolerating unstructured mental space is a genuine relational skill — most people fill every gap with reassurance-seeking and never let a thought actually finish arriving. Letting a worry pass through unanswered, even once, for a few counted breaths, restores the same open space your best insight actually comes from. The gap you're avoiding is the resource, not the threat."
  },
  "25": {
    "wiring": "You absorb a great deal of life without needing it sanded down first, and relationships keep handing you precisely the things you didn't choose. You take a person as they actually are and still say clearly what it is you need from them. You give the people closest to you the ongoing experience of not being edited.",
    "root": "Something early gave you every reason to conclude that opening your heart was exactly how the original wound got reopened, and instead of confirming that fear you built the opposite capacity — staying open anyway. That's not a small thing to have built, and it came at real cost. What looks effortless now was earned the hard way.",
    "defaultMode": "The tightening arrives the moment somebody gets near enough to reach the tender place in you — a sharp remark, a sudden distance, a day where your warmth switches off with no explanation offered. You test people rather than tell them what hurt, then read their confusion as further proof that closeness was never safe.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how selectively you're actually being open, because the guarded parts feel invisible from inside — you experience yourself as accepting, full stop. Others notice the specific places you go quiet or distant that you might not register as a pattern. What you call being fine sometimes reads to people close to you as a door quietly shutting.",
    "underusedStrength": "Full acceptance of a difficult reality, without needing it to change first, is a real capacity most people never develop, and it's precisely why people trust you with things they can't say elsewhere. Naming, out loud, one thing you usually withhold in a specific relationship converts your general openness into something the other person can actually feel directed at them. The capacity is already built; it just needs to be aimed at a specific person instead of held in general."
  },
  "26": {
    "wiring": "You read what will persuade somebody within the first minute of meeting them, and relationships keep testing whether that skill serves them or only ever serves you. You frame a hard truth so that it's actually able to be received. You admit being wrong without collapsing into apology.",
    "root": "The unimpressive, ordinary truth about you felt early on like it wouldn't be enough to keep anybody around, so managing how you were perceived became a habit that ran alongside — sometimes ahead of — the actual truth. That fear is old and still shapes how much of the unedited version of you gets shown. It formed before you'd tested what happened when the plain version was actually seen.",
    "defaultMode": "You sell the version of events that keeps you looking capable, and the people closest to you sense the gap without being able to name it. You push past a no whenever you believe you know better, and afterwards call it enthusiasm rather than the pressure it was.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see the gap between the version of you that's being managed for effect and the version that's actually present. Others sense they're being handled rather than met before you'd admit that's what's happening. What feels like being persuasive to you sometimes reads to them as being worked on.",
    "underusedStrength": "Reading what someone needs to hear in order to actually receive a hard truth is genuine craft, not manipulation, once it's aimed at their good rather than your own advantage. Telling someone the unpolished version of something, once, without the usual performance around it, deepens trust in a way the charm alone never manages. The skill of sideways delivery is real; it just needs an occasional direct exception to stay honest."
  },
  "27": {
    "wiring": "You give care with a practical hand, and life keeps arranging situations that ask you who is actually being fed by all of it. You notice who is quietly struggling in a busy room long before that person says a word. You check your own tank first, more than you used to.",
    "root": "Providing for others was how you secured your own place in a group or family early on, so giving became both genuine care and a strategy for belonging, tangled together before you could separate the two. That equation is old and still runs underneath most of your close relationships. It formed before anyone taught you that you'd be wanted without it.",
    "defaultMode": "You give until you're completely empty and then take that emptiness out on everyone in the house. You feed other people's needs while neglecting sleep, food, and your own boundaries, then treat that depletion as the main evidence of your worth. Beneath it is a belief that your own needs are an imposition, so you never state them and quietly hope somebody guesses.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how rarely you ask what you actually need compared to how consistently you meet everyone else's. Others notice you giving well past the point of your own comfort before you're willing to name it as a pattern. What looks like generosity from outside is sometimes, up close, a quiet form of self-neglect.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine giving, without an attached expectation, is rare and it's why people trust you specifically — but receiving is the underused half of the same skill. Naming what actually replenishes you, and scheduling it like an obligation instead of leaving it optional, is the practical fix that lets the giving continue without draining you dry. The instinct that serves others so well works on you too, once you let it."
  },
  "28": {
    "wiring": "You enter things wholly, and the recurring lesson keeps arriving through people who would prefer you running at half speed instead. You talk about death, endings, and meaning without flinching. You leave people feeling more alive for having risked more than they planned to around you.",
    "root": "Loss or intensity encountered early either forced this capacity to develop or showed you that avoidance doesn't actually protect anyone from anything. Facing hard things directly was simply necessary before it became a strength. The comfort with intensity was built under real conditions, not chosen abstractly.",
    "defaultMode": "A flatness arrives that no achievement seems able to touch for very long. You chase intensity through risk, through crisis, or through relationships that keep you permanently at the edge, because calm registers as absence rather than peace. You start things wholeheartedly and abandon them the moment the meaning drains out, leaving other people holding whatever it was you began.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see, from inside the chase, that intensity is substituting for meaning rather than producing it. Others notice the pattern of abandoned pursuits before you're willing to call it a pattern. What feels like following your instincts sometimes reads, from outside, as running from stillness.",
    "underusedStrength": "The willingness to go fully into difficult territory that most people avoid is a genuinely rare capacity, and it makes you useful to people in crisis in a way few others can be. Giving one commitment your full weight, instead of splitting your intensity across several, produces the sense of significance you're actually chasing through the gamble. The capacity for full engagement is already built; it just needs a single target instead of many."
  },
  "29": {
    "wiring": "You commit with your whole body once you've actually said yes, and relationships keep teaching you which yes is genuinely yours to give. You stay through the unglamorous middle of things, long after the people who started alongside you have quietly drifted off. Your word functions as a finished thing, just waiting for its date.",
    "root": "Half-commitments produced worse outcomes early on than full ones, or being reliable — actually following through — distinguished you in an environment where that wasn't the norm. Full commitment became both a value and a practical strategy that worked. The habit of staying in through the middle is old and tested.",
    "defaultMode": "A yes gets given before you've checked whether you actually meant it. You're present in body and entirely absent in every other way, and the person beside you feels the difference immediately. You over-promise to keep the peace, then leak your reluctance through lateness, distraction, and a tone you'd deny having used.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily tell, in the moment you say yes, whether you're actually in or performing being in. Others sometimes feel the half-heartedness before you're willing to admit it exists. What you experience as being agreeable sometimes reads to them as being unreliable in a way that's hard to name.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine, whole-body commitment is rare, and it's why people build their own plans around your word without asking twice. Checking honestly whether a yes is a full yes before you give it, not after you've already regretted it, protects both the relationship and your own energy. The instinct for total commitment is already there; the discipline is knowing when to withhold it."
  },
  "30": {
    "wiring": "You lighten whatever other people are carrying heavily, and life keeps handing you longings that flatly refuse to resolve on demand. You feel a desire fully, let it move right through you, and stay warm rather than grim about it. You laugh in the middle of real difficulty without making someone else's pain small.",
    "root": "Satisfaction felt like it was always one arrival further on early on, never anywhere you already stood, and that conviction got reinforced by how often the next thing genuinely did deliver a rush the current thing had stopped giving. Wanting became the engine; arriving became a brief pause before wanting resumed. That loop is old.",
    "defaultMode": "A wanting that will not sit still fixes on a person, a future, or a feeling, and the present moment becomes merely the waiting room in your head for whatever comes next. In relationships you burn hot at the start and cool when the fantasy meets an ordinary Tuesday, and the other person feels that temperature drop.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of your want has already moved on to its next object before you've consciously registered leaving the last one. Others notice the cooling before you'd admit you were already elsewhere. What looks like ambition or appetite from outside is sometimes just an old restlessness with a new face.",
    "underusedStrength": "Feeling desire fully, without needing it satisfied instantly, is a rare relational skill most people either suppress or get ruled by. Naming the want honestly, out loud, rather than acting it out or hiding it, keeps you accountable to it instead of chasing its next disguise. This is closeness with the longing named instead of just followed."
  },
  "31": {
    "wiring": "You speak and people turn toward you, and the recurring test is whether what you said came from the whole group or only from you. You change your position publicly the moment somebody shows you something better. You give other people permission to think out loud and be wrong in your company.",
    "root": "Your worth got tied early on to being the one with the answer, so silence in a group started to feel like a kind of disappearing rather than simply not needing to speak yet. That equation is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether you'd still matter without the answer.",
    "defaultMode": "You speak for a group you never consulted, and you mistake the room going quiet for the room agreeing with you. In close relationships you win the argument and lose the person, then feel genuinely puzzled by the coolness that follows you home.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see the moment your voice stopped representing the room and started representing only you. Others notice the shift from shared leadership to something closer to imposition before you do. What feels like clarity to you sometimes reads to them as being talked over.",
    "underusedStrength": "The ability to voice what everybody already half-knows is genuinely rare, and every correction you've accepted along the way has tuned that ear further. Checking your position with someone before you commit to it publicly gives them permission to think out loud and be wrong in your company too. Most people with this ability can't resist filling every gap with more of themselves, which is exactly what makes yours land better when you don't."
  },
  "32": {
    "wiring": "You preserve what is genuinely worth keeping, and life keeps testing your judgement about when to hold on tight and when to let something finally end. You sense which relationship, plan, or tradition has bones in it and which has gone hollow. You stay with people right through their worst seasons.",
    "root": "One visible failure felt early on like it would define you permanently in everyone else's eyes, so avoiding it took priority over testing your own actual capability. That fear is old and still shapes how much you risk in close relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether a single failure actually cost what you feared.",
    "defaultMode": "You cling to arrangements long past their natural life — a friendship, a habit, a relationship whose season ended years ago. You also refuse to begin things you're not certain of finishing, and then call that refusal prudence rather than fear.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much you've stayed with, past its natural end, in service of avoiding the appearance of failure rather than actually protecting anything worth protecting. Others notice you holding on before you'd admit the season had passed. What feels like loyalty to you sometimes reads to them as being unable to let go.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine judgment about what's actually durable is a rare relational asset most people never develop, since most confuse comfort with quality. Testing your own readiness once, in a relationship or commitment you've been circling, rather than gathering one more year of certainty, puts real capability where the people who love you can actually feel it. The caution was never wrong; it just needed an occasional exception."
  },
  "33": {
    "wiring": "You retain the thread of a relationship even after stepping away from it for weeks, and you pick it up exactly where it broke. You bring a mindfulness to conflict most people work at for years — noticing the precise moment a conversation turned. You come back from your retreats carrying something the other person couldn't have reached alone.",
    "root": "Remembering the last rupture in full felt like it would cost more than you could survive twice, so forgetting became protective — a way to stay comfortable that quietly removed the one thing intimacy actually runs on. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest bonds. It formed before you'd tested whether remembering could actually be survived.",
    "defaultMode": "Closeness gets heavy, and you forget — the argument, the promise, the look on their face. Withdrawal begins as a sane need for space and hardens into a habit of vanishing whenever someone asks for more than you have that day. The same lesson returns in a new relationship wearing new clothes, and you meet it with the same blank.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how consistently you go blank right at the point a relationship needs continuity most. Others carry the history alone before you'd admit you'd dropped your half of it. What feels like needing space to you sometimes reads to them as being erased from the record.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to retain a relationship's full thread across a retreat is a genuinely rare asset most people lose the moment they need distance. Writing down or naming the thing you'd normally let go quiet before you withdraw, rather than trusting memory to hold it through the gap, converts your natural recall into something the relationship can actually rely on. The skill is intact; the discipline is capturing it before the withdrawal erases it."
  },
  "34": {
    "wiring": "You anchor real strength into rooms where other people are still deciding whether to act, and they start moving because you moved first. You hold a steadiness under pressure that the people closest to you lean on without saying so. You keep your power fully available and your hands entirely open.",
    "root": "Your value got tied early on to the volume of what you could carry, so pushing through and driving outcomes became the reflex rather than one option among several. That equation is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether people would stay without the constant proof of your capacity.",
    "defaultMode": "You push a decision through, override a slower voice, finish a sentence that wasn't yours, and the room goes compliant rather than willing. The fear underneath is that if you stop driving, nothing happens at all and everyone quietly drifts away. What you lose is not authority but company — people stay near your energy and stop bringing you the fragile things.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much your force is costing you in terms of what people stop bringing you once they've been overridden a few times. Others notice they've quietly stopped offering the fragile things before you'd admit they'd stopped. What feels like helping to you sometimes reads to them as being steamrolled.",
    "underusedStrength": "Real strength, offered without needing an audience to confirm it, is genuinely rare and it's exactly what makes people trust you with the hardest moments. Waiting one beat before acting on someone else's hesitation, instead of moving through it, tests whether your power is actually needed or just reflexively deployed. The strength was never the problem; the timing of its use was."
  },
  "35": {
    "wiring": "You remain awake to possibility in relationships other people have already filed as settled, and that keeps things alive around you years after the excitement was supposed to be over. You propose the trip, the move, the honest conversation nobody else would open. You bring hunger toward someone instead of away from them.",
    "root": "Stillness felt early on like it meant the end of you, so changing the scenery became a way to prove you were still alive rather than a genuine response to anything actually stale. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether staying still was actually survivable.",
    "defaultMode": "A relationship reaches the ordinary stretch after the arrival, and you start scanning for the next thing while the person in front of you is still mid-sentence. They feel the shift long before you admit it. The same restlessness meets you in the next place, in the next person, and you begin to suspect the pattern travels with you rather than the circumstances.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that the restlessness is coming from inside you rather than from the relationship actually going stale. Others notice you scanning for exits before you'd admit that's what you were doing. What feels like following opportunity to you sometimes reads to them as never quite arriving anywhere.",
    "underusedStrength": "The appetite for real aliveness is a genuine relational asset most people lose somewhere in their twenties and never recover. Bringing that hunger toward the person already in front of you — proposing the trip, the honest conversation, the new territory, together — is more interesting than anything left behind. The restlessness was never wrong; aiming it outward instead of away is the fix."
  },
  "36": {
    "wiring": "You match other people at the exact depth of what they're going through, and they stop performing within minutes of sitting with you. You hear someone's worst confession without flinching, changing the subject, or rushing to fix it. You turn your own difficult history into something usable for the people beside you.",
    "root": "Calm registered early on as absence rather than peace, so you learned to associate intensity with actually being alive and stillness with something closer to disappearing. That association is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether a calm connection could feel like anything other than nothing.",
    "defaultMode": "You're drawn to intensity, to the crisis that needs you, to relationships that run hot enough to feel like proof of life. Calm registers as absence, so you unsettle a steady bond just to feel the current again, and the steady person leaves. Underneath is a suspicion that you're only lovable while something is going wrong and you're the one holding it together.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that you're the one generating the turbulence that later feels like it happened to you. Others notice the pattern of manufactured crisis before you'd call it that yourself. What feels like bad luck to you sometimes reads to them as a preference for chaos over calm.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to sit with someone's worst moment without flinching is a genuinely rare relational skill in a world that mostly avoids hard feeling or manages it badly. Offering that same steadiness in an ordinary, uneventful week, not just a crisis one, is what actually lets a calm bond feel alive instead of empty. The depth was never the problem; where it gets aimed is."
  },
  "37": {
    "wiring": "You radiate warmth freely inside your closest circle, and people organise their sense of home around the fact that you're in it. You hold family and friendship together through practical acts nobody else remembers. You insist on equality, refusing to let anyone in the group be treated as lesser.",
    "root": "Affection felt conditional early on — earned rather than given — so keeping the peace at home by giving past the point of gladness became a habit built to protect the relationship from ever testing that condition. That fear is old and still runs underneath your closest bonds. It formed before you'd tested whether the affection would hold without the constant giving.",
    "defaultMode": "You agree to terms you never wanted, give past the point of gladness, and call it love while the resentment builds somewhere you can't see it. The same imbalance repeats with a new partner or a new circle, and you find yourself once again doing the emotional work of two people.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of your own terms you've quietly given away to keep a relationship comfortable. Others notice the accommodation before you'd name it as a pattern yourself. What you call being easy to love sometimes reads to them as being taken for granted on purpose.",
    "underusedStrength": "Building genuinely warm, equal relationships is a rare gift most people never fully develop. Naming a fair term plainly, before resentment builds, keeps the warmth intact instead of quietly eroding it. The skill of making people feel like family is real; it just needs to include you in the accounting."
  },
  "38": {
    "wiring": "You persist in relationships long after the point where most people would have quietly withdrawn their effort. You bring a perseverance that's been earned rather than inherited. You spend that fight only on what actually matters most to you.",
    "root": "Anything given without resistance felt early on like it couldn't be trusted, so proving people through friction became a way to test whether their commitment was real. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether ease could also be trustworthy.",
    "defaultMode": "Struggle becomes the shape you expect a relationship to have, and ease starts to feel like something is missing. You look for the fight, take the opposing side, push back on a suggestion before you've finished hearing it. The belief underneath is that anything given without resistance is not real, so you make people prove themselves.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when you've manufactured the resistance you're now spending real effort overcoming. Others notice the fight being started before you'd admit you'd started it. What feels like testing commitment to you sometimes reads to them as picking a fight for its own sake.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine perseverance on what actually matters is a rare trait most people admire and few can match. Letting ease register as trustworthy, on purpose, when a relationship is actually going well, lets the skill work without needing manufactured conflict to justify it. The strength was never the struggle; it was the staying power underneath it."
  },
  "39": {
    "wiring": "You wake people up simply by being in the room with them, and the conversation gets more honest than it was going to be. You ask the question everyone was circling and the whole thing moves. You aim that charge at what genuinely needs freeing rather than at whoever is nearest.",
    "root": "A relationship only felt like it had proven itself once it had been tested under real stress, so you learned to apply pressure yourself rather than wait for stress to arrive on its own. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest bonds. It formed before you'd tested whether ease could also be trusted as real.",
    "defaultMode": "You poke at a partner's soft spot, escalate a small disagreement, say the accurate cruel thing because the tension had to go somewhere. The belief beneath is that a relationship only proves itself under stress, so you keep applying stress to check the structure. What you lose is the other person's openness, since nobody stays soft around someone who tests them for sport.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily tell when you've stopped provoking with purpose and started provoking out of your own unspent restlessness. Others notice the difference between purposeful pressure and reflexive needling before you're willing to separate the two. What feels like checking the relationship's strength to you sometimes reads to them as being treated as a target.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to locate and release stuck relational energy is a genuinely rare skill most conflict-avoidant people desperately need and actively resist. Moving your own restlessness through physical exertion before you speak, rather than discharging it verbally at whoever's nearest, keeps the skill aimed at what actually needs unsticking. The nerve was never the problem; the target was."
  },
  "40": {
    "wiring": "You know your own limits with a clarity most people never develop, and you say no while they're still working out how to phrase it. Your commitment survives whole seasons of inconvenience without wobbling. You've learned that your rest is not a withdrawal of love.",
    "root": "Your worth got measured early on by what you delivered for other people, so stopping felt like disappearing rather than resting, and needing anything for yourself started to feel like a defect. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether you'd still be wanted with real limits in place.",
    "defaultMode": "You take on the load for a family, a partner, a friend, and you carry it well past the point where anyone would still expect it. Then the refusal arrives all at once and lands as coldness — a door closed, a friendship dropped, a partner blindsided by an ending you'd been rehearsing alone for months.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how far past your actual capacity you've been operating, because the identity of being reliable makes the depletion invisible to you specifically. Others notice the strain building before you'd admit it yourself. What you experience as commitment sometimes reads to them as someone quietly running on empty.",
    "underusedStrength": "Knowing your limits with real clarity, and saying no early rather than late, is a genuine relational advantage that protects both the relationship and your own capacity. Stating the line before you've crossed it, rather than after the resentment has built, gives people a real chance to meet the actual you instead of the sudden withdrawal. Most people never develop this level of honesty about their own limits."
  },
  "41": {
    "wiring": "You sense what's coming in a relationship before there's anything visible to point at, and you're right about it far more than you give yourself credit for. You feel a new chapter arriving and prepare the ground before anyone else notices the shift. You've learned to say the feeling out loud rather than sit alone with it.",
    "root": "Longing itself became the pleasure early on, so choosing what was out of reach felt safer and more alive than committing to whoever was genuinely available. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether the ordinary, present version could actually satisfy you.",
    "defaultMode": "You build the whole relationship in your head — the conversation, their answer, the life that follows — and then meet the actual person as a disappointment. The fear is that ordinary presence won't be enough for you, and that the real thing will always be smaller than the version you imagined.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of the relationship you're living privately, in anticipation, instead of with the actual person in front of you. Others sense the gap between your imagined version of them and their real self before you'd admit it exists. What feels like being in love to you sometimes is love directed at someone who isn't fully there.",
    "underusedStrength": "Sensing what's coming before it's visible is a genuine relational gift most people never develop. Staying with the actual, present person past the point where the anticipation usually takes over lets the real chapter arrive instead of the imagined one. The instinct for what's coming was never wrong; living there instead of in the present was."
  },
  "42": {
    "wiring": "You release people and situations at the right moment, and the ones who've watched you do it are quietly grateful for the cleanness of it. You let a phase of a relationship end without demanding it stay the shape it was. You stay fully present through an ending and still love the person on the way out of it.",
    "root": "An unfinished thing felt early on like it couldn't be taken from you, so leaving doors open — even ones you'd stopped wanting to walk through — became a way to protect against loss rather than a genuine choice. That belief is old and still runs underneath how you handle endings now. It formed before you'd tested whether a clean ending was actually survivable.",
    "defaultMode": "You decide privately how something should conclude, then feel obscurely wronged when the other person lives their own version instead. Endings get dragged out — the conversation that will not finish, the friendship kept on life support because closing it would mean the story went badly.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much energy is going into an ending you're privately prolonging rather than actually resolving. Others notice the door left open before you'd admit you were the one holding it. What feels like keeping options alive to you sometimes reads to them as being unable to actually finish anything.",
    "underusedStrength": "Recognizing when something is genuinely finished, and closing it cleanly, is a rare relational skill in a culture that mostly leaves things half-open indefinitely. Naming the ending plainly, the moment you actually know it, rather than after months of quiet extension, frees the energy for what's actually next. The instinct for clean closure is already built; it just needs to be trusted sooner."
  },
  "43": {
    "wiring": "You catch the shape of a problem before anyone has finished explaining it, and your insight arrives whole rather than assembling itself in pieces. You name the real issue while others are still discussing the surface one. You wait for the moment someone is actually able to receive what you see.",
    "root": "Being interrupted felt early on like being overwritten, so defending your inner space by shutting the door on people who questioned you became protective rather than isolating on purpose. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether being interrupted was actually as costly as it felt.",
    "defaultMode": "You go so far inside your own reasoning that the person speaking becomes background noise, and they eventually stop trying to reach you. Then you deliver the conclusion at the wrong moment, in language nobody asked for, and it lands as arrogance rather than help.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how often you've stopped listening well before you've stopped speaking, because the internal reasoning feels like engagement from the inside. Others notice they've been talking to themselves for a while before you'd admit you'd checked out. What feels like depth to you sometimes reads to them as absence.",
    "underusedStrength": "Insight that arrives whole between two people is genuinely rare, and it lands best offered gradually rather than delivered complete and unannounced. Walking through even one step of how you got there, instead of presenting only the conclusion, is what actually lets someone stay with you instead of being talked past. This is knowing with nothing left in the way of being shared."
  },
  "44": {
    "wiring": "You assemble working relationships out of people who wouldn't otherwise have found each other, and the results outlast your involvement by years. You sense within one meeting how someone will behave under pressure, and you place them accordingly. You trust the read without weaponising it.",
    "root": "Past betrayals taught you early on that people repeat their patterns and can't be trusted to surprise you, and that lesson generalized into a habit of pre-empting the next disappointment by controlling the terms of every alliance you're part of. That belief is old and still runs underneath how you approach new people. It formed before you'd tested whether people could actually surprise you.",
    "defaultMode": "You manage the connections around you — a word here, a warning there — and call it care while quietly steering who gets close to whom. The belief underneath is that people repeat their patterns and cannot be trusted to surprise you, which makes it impossible for anyone to prove otherwise.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when your reading of someone has become a fixed prediction you're now unconsciously steering them toward fulfilling. Others notice the suspicion before you'd name it as suspicion yourself. What feels like protecting the group to you sometimes reads to them as never quite being trusted.",
    "underusedStrength": "The instinct for reading people accurately is a rare relational talent most people never get access to. Letting one relationship unfold without the usual pre-emptive management, on purpose, tests whether your read was protection or just an old prediction running the show. The skill was never in question; the suspicion attached to it was."
  },
  "45": {
    "wiring": "You gather people into a shared effort simply by describing it clearly, and they contribute more than they meant to. You make each person's contribution visible, and the whole thing works better for it. You've learned that the abundance you want arrives faster through people than around them.",
    "root": "Being clearly the centre of things felt early on like the only guarantee of not being left out, so being generous became tangled up with staying visibly indispensable. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether you'd be kept without constantly proving your usefulness.",
    "defaultMode": "You take the decision back, speak over the quieter voice, subtly rank the people around you by usefulness to what you're building. Underneath is a fear of scarcity — that if you're not clearly the centre, you'll be left out of what gets shared. The same complaint recurs across teams, friendships, and family: that you're generous but not really open.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when gathering people has quietly become keeping them positioned around you rather than actually letting them lead. Others notice the subtle ranking before you'd call it that yourself. What feels like generosity to you sometimes reads to them as strategic centring.",
    "underusedStrength": "The ability to gather people into a shared effort and make their contributions visible is a genuinely rare relational advantage. Letting the group move without you occasionally, on purpose, tests whether the connection you built is real or just your presence holding the centre. Most people who can convene this well never risk finding out."
  },
  "46": {
    "wiring": "You take delight in situations other people are still bracing against, and the mood in the room turns because of it. You make room for joy in the middle of a difficult week, and everyone breathes. You've learned your enjoyment of things is contagious rather than frivolous.",
    "root": "Lightness felt early on like it wasn't safe — like relaxing would mean being caught unprepared by whatever was coming next — so heaviness became a way to feel in control of a relationship that actually mattered. That belief is old and still runs underneath how you handle closeness now. It formed before you'd tested whether ease was actually more dangerous than vigilance.",
    "defaultMode": "You grow heavy and dutiful, treat love as a project to be managed well, and lose the pleasure of the person entirely. The body gets ignored in the same move — closeness becomes something you think about rather than something you actually enjoy having.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when caring about a relationship has quietly turned into managing it rather than actually enjoying it. Others feel graded rather than enjoyed before you'd admit that's the shift that happened. What feels like taking care of the relationship to you sometimes reads to them as being under review.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine delight, offered without a defensive weight around it, is a rare relational skill most people lose the moment something starts to matter. Letting yourself enjoy the person in front of you on purpose, especially when the stakes feel high, restores exactly the ease that would otherwise drain out while you're busy taking care of things. The lightness was never the risk; the vigilance replacing it was."
  },
  "47": {
    "wiring": "You turn difficult experience into something usable, and other people come to you specifically because you've been through the dark and come back able to describe it. You sit with someone in confusion without needing it resolved by the end of the conversation. Your own hard years were not wasted.",
    "root": "A descent into difficulty at some point felt like it revealed who you actually were, and everything brighter felt borrowed by comparison, so the dark material got treated as more true than the good stretches. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether both could be equally real.",
    "defaultMode": "You sink, go dark, disappear into a mood that has no visible cause, and the person beside you is left guessing what they did. Because you can't explain it while it's happening, you say nothing, and your silence gets read as judgement or as leaving.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see, while in the descent, that your silence is being read as something about the other person rather than as your own internal weather passing through. Others build a story about what they did wrong before you'd think to correct it. What feels private to you sometimes reads to them as being shut out.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to sit with someone's confusion without needing it resolved is a genuine relational gift most people can't offer because they fear their own mess too much to tolerate someone else's. Naming the descent out loud, even briefly — 'this isn't about you, I'm somewhere dark right now' — keeps the relationship intact through the very stretches that used to isolate you both. The depth was never wasted; keeping it silent is what cost you."
  },
  "48": {
    "wiring": "You make workable answers out of situations that have no precedent, and people bring you their genuinely unsolvable problems for exactly that reason. You show real resourcefulness with incomplete information and almost no time to prepare. Your best work begins in not knowing.",
    "root": "Your depth felt fraudulent early on, like it might not survive one real question, so measuring yourself against everything you hadn't yet mastered became the habit instead of crediting what you plainly already knew. That belief is old and still shapes how much of yourself you let people see. It formed before you'd tested whether the depth was actually as fragile as it felt.",
    "defaultMode": "You hold back the contribution, decline the role, stay quiet in the conversation you had the most to offer, and afterwards you hear someone else say your thought. In relationships it becomes a slow test — you wait for the other person to discover the shortfall you're certain is there, and you leave first to avoid it.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much you're already trusted for a depth you keep insisting isn't real. Others notice the gap between your actual capability and your visible confidence before you'd close it yourself. What feels like humility to you sometimes reads to them as reluctance to be fully known.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine depth, built quietly over years, is a rare relational resource that only benefits the people close to you once you actually let it be seen. Saying the thing you're holding back, in the moment you're having it, is the practical fix — readiness was never actually the missing ingredient. The capacity has been there for a while; the permission is the only thing lagging behind it."
  },
  "49": {
    "wiring": "You register an accurate sense of when a relationship has to change, and the people closest to you rely on that reading far more than they say. You raise the hard thing early, while it's still an ordinary conversation rather than a rupture. You can end something cleanly and still wish the other person well.",
    "root": "Being discarded felt early on like the worst possible outcome, so striking first — ending things before you could be the one left — became a way to control an old fear rather than a genuine response to what was in front of you. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether staying through friction was actually survivable.",
    "defaultMode": "Someone crosses a line and the whole bond is on trial within a single minute. You cut people off at full speed, then discover later that the offence was much smaller than the ending you built on top of it. Other times you swing the other way and absorb everything in silence for a year, until one ordinary remark detonates.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when you've ended something at the first sign of trouble rather than after actually testing whether it could survive the friction. Others notice the speed of your exits before you'd call it a pattern yourself. What feels like having standards to you sometimes reads to them as never getting a real chance.",
    "underusedStrength": "The instinct to end what's genuinely finished, cleanly, is a rare relational skill most people never develop, staying instead in relationships long past their honest end. Testing whether an offence is actually fatal, rather than assuming it is, lets you use the skill on what's genuinely over instead of on the first sign of friction. The clarity about endings was never wrong; the trigger for using it was too sensitive."
  },
  "50": {
    "wiring": "You guard the standard in your relationships, and people feel the difference between your rooms and everybody else's within an hour of walking into one. You sense imbalance early, noticing when one person in a family or team is carrying too much before that person says a word. You adjust the load and let the whole group settle back into something workable.",
    "root": "You became responsible for holding everyone together early on, whether or not anyone actually assigned you that role, and the belief that any collapse anywhere would be your own failure got built before you learned you could ask for help holding it. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships.",
    "defaultMode": "You keep a rule you no longer believe in because dismantling it would upset people, or let a friend's behaviour slide because confronting it would cost you the peace you've worked for. You become the guardian of an order that has quietly gone rotten, and you defend it hardest when someone younger questions it.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of the group's balance you've quietly taken sole responsibility for. Others notice you carrying weight nobody handed you before you'd admit you'd picked it up. What you call diligence sometimes reads to them as you not trusting anyone else with the standard.",
    "underusedStrength": "The instinct for what a healthy relationship or family actually requires is a genuine relational asset most people lack entirely. Naming a drifting standard out loud the first time you notice it, rather than quietly absorbing the correction yourself, distributes the responsibility instead of accumulating all of it in you. The judgment was never the problem; carrying it alone was."
  },
  "51": {
    "wiring": "You weather shocks better than almost anyone you know, and the people around you have noticed you're the one who steadies quickest when the ground moves. You take the first step into a difficult conversation while everyone else is still circling it. You recover fast from being challenged, treating a hard truth about yourself as useful information.",
    "root": "Mattering felt tied early on to being noticed in the middle of something urgent, so a calm, stable relationship started to feel like being invisible rather than simply being safe. That association is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether calm could actually be noticed too.",
    "defaultMode": "You provoke, interrupt, or escalate because a level room feels like a dead room to you, and afterwards you wonder why partners describe you as hard to relax around. You compete where no competition was ever offered, turning a friend's good news into a private scoreboard you'd never admit to keeping.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when you've manufactured the disruption you then respond to so capably. Others notice the shock arriving right when things were calm before you'd admit you'd caused it. What feels like initiative to you sometimes reads to them as being hard to relax around.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine resilience under real shock is a rare relational asset, and it produces the most trust when it's available for actual crises rather than manufactured ones. Letting a calm stretch stay calm, on purpose, without testing it, lets the people who love you feel safe instead of braced. The steadiness was never the problem; the appetite for testing it was."
  },
  "52": {
    "wiring": "You wait well, and that single quiet capacity has defused far more difficult situations in your life than any argument you've ever won on the facts. You hold your response when a conversation heats up, giving the other person room to hear their own words. Your restraint is active and deliberate, not passive.",
    "root": "Any movement made under pressure felt early on like it would turn out to be the wrong one, so no movement started to feel safest — containment rather than selective release. That belief is old and still runs underneath how you handle conflict now. It formed before you'd tested whether some movement was actually safe.",
    "defaultMode": "You freeze in the middle of a conflict, saying nothing while the other person waits, and your silence reads to them as contempt rather than as the overload it actually is. Then you withdraw to recover and stay withdrawn for far longer than the situation ever required.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that your silence is being read as rejection rather than as the internal overload you're actually experiencing. Others fill your quiet with a story about you that's worse than the truth before you'd think to correct it. What feels like self-protection to you sometimes reads to them as contempt.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to wait, actively and deliberately, is a rare relational skill in a culture that rewards instant reaction. Naming the overload out loud — 'I need a minute, this isn't contempt' — the moment you go quiet, rather than letting the silence speak for itself, keeps the restraint working for the relationship instead of against it. Most people never develop this level of patience; the fix is giving it a voice."
  },
  "53": {
    "wiring": "You grow through people, and the relationships in your life have consistently been the doorway into whatever came next for you. You bring a beginner's open willingness into rooms full of people busy protecting their expertise. When your world widens, you pull the people beside you into the new territory rather than leaving them behind.",
    "root": "Staying through a relationship's difficult middle felt early on like it would reveal you as ordinary, while the arrival — the beginning — stayed safe from that verdict. That belief is old and still shapes how deep your relationships go. It formed before you'd tested whether the middle was actually as exposing as it felt.",
    "defaultMode": "You begin brilliantly, then feel the pull of the next thing exactly when the relationship, project, or family stage asks for endurance instead of enthusiasm. People close to you learn to hold back their investment, because they've watched you start with them and then finish somewhere else entirely.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how consistently you leave right before a relationship could actually prove itself past the beginning. Others notice the pattern of promising starts and quiet departures before you're willing to name it yourself. What feels like following growth to you sometimes reads to them as an inability to stay for the unglamorous part.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to grow rapidly through new relationships is a genuine gift most people lose access to by midlife. Staying through one relationship's difficult middle, on purpose, using the same openness you already bring to beginnings, converts your natural growth instinct into something that actually deepens instead of just restarting. The instinct for beginnings was never wrong; it just needed a second act."
  },
  "54": {
    "wiring": "You climb, and the people who know you well have watched you build something real out of beginnings nobody else would have bothered with. You bring drive into your relationships as loyalty in motion, showing up with practical help rather than sympathy. Your stamina has survived every setback so far.",
    "root": "You learned early that you were only worth loving at the top of something, so ambition and the need to be seen as capable got tangled together with the actual desire for connection. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether you'd be loved without the constant proof of climbing.",
    "defaultMode": "You measure your friendships against your climb without ever meaning to, and the ones who cannot help you feel the temperature drop. When the ambition stalls you become punishing company, treating a partner's ordinary happiness as evidence they've settled for far less.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much you've filtered your closest relationships by their usefulness to your own advancement. Others feel the temperature drop before you'd admit you'd caused it. What feels like being focused to you sometimes reads to them as being used as a rung.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine drive, offered as loyalty in motion rather than personal ambition, is a rare relational asset that shows up as real, practical help for the people you love. Checking honestly whether a friendship is being valued for itself or for what it can offer your climb keeps the ambition pointed at something worth having reached the top for. The stamina was never the problem; the target occasionally needs re-checking."
  },
  "55": {
    "wiring": "You outgrow arrangements that no longer fit you, and you've done it repeatedly while the people around you stayed exactly where they were. You feel your own emotional weather with unusual accuracy, and you've stopped pretending to be level on days when you plainly aren't. You refuse the small unspoken contracts most couples sign without reading.",
    "root": "Your feelings got treated early on as something being done to you by other people, rather than as weather passing through you on its own schedule, so blame became easier to reach for than acknowledging the feeling was simply yours to move through. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships.",
    "defaultMode": "Their mood arrives and you call it your ruin, then punish them for a weather system that neither of you controls. You wait to be rescued from a situation you have full power to leave, and the waiting curdles into blame that comes out sideways for months.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see, from inside a low stretch, that the feeling is passing weather rather than evidence about the relationship or the person you're with. Others notice the blame arriving before you'd separate mood from fact yourself. What feels like clarity to you in that state sometimes reads to them as being made responsible for your weather.",
    "underusedStrength": "Recognizing your own emotional weather as passing, rather than caused by someone else, is a genuine relational advantage most people never develop. Naming the feeling as yours, out loud, the moment it arrives — 'this is my weather, not about you' — keeps the relationship honest instead of turning you into a case against them. The honesty about your own state was never the problem; blaming it outward was."
  },
  "56": {
    "wiring": "You collect experience the way other people gather possessions, and the ones closest to you live noticeably wider lives for having known you. You bring stories, food, music, and interesting strangers into your relationships. You notice what would delight one specific person and go get it for them.",
    "root": "Stillness with one person felt early on like it might turn out to be empty, so novelty became a way to keep testing whether you'd be the one to find that out, rather than staying to see. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether one person, sustained, could actually be enough.",
    "defaultMode": "You leave a hard conversation for a more entertaining one, changing the subject with such charm that neither of you notices the avoidance until the same issue returns a month later. You collect new people while the older ones wait their turn, mistaking novelty for growth and appetite for intimacy.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of your gathering is escaping the depth available with the people you already have rather than actually seeking growth. Others notice the pattern of novelty over depth before you'd name it yourself. What feels like curiosity to you sometimes reads to them as never quite staying still with anyone.",
    "underusedStrength": "The ability to gather rich experience and bring it into a relationship is a genuine gift most people never develop. Staying through one hard conversation to its actual end, instead of pivoting to something more entertaining, lets the depth you're capable of curating for others show up in your closest bond too. The appetite was never the problem; the avoidance riding along with it was."
  },
  "57": {
    "wiring": "You track what's happening in a room long before anybody explains it, and you've been right about people far more than you give yourself credit for. You pick up the shift in someone's voice on the phone and act on it. Your instinct works fastest at close range.",
    "root": "A feeling without a reason felt early on like it didn't count, so you learned to override or discard your most accurate instrument rather than trust something you couldn't yet justify. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested how often the instinct was actually right.",
    "defaultMode": "You stay in situations your nervous system flagged in the very first week, explaining the dread away because the person was kind and you had no hard evidence. Loud environments and forceful people flood you completely, and you agree to things in that state you'd never agree to alone at home.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how often your instinct was right after the fact, because you discarded it before it had a chance to be tested openly. Others notice your read was accurate in hindsight more often than you give yourself credit for. What feels like uncertainty to you sometimes was actually clear information you didn't trust yet.",
    "underusedStrength": "Fast, accurate intuition about people is a genuine relational asset most people never develop this precisely. Naming the instinct out loud as a hypothesis, even without evidence yet — 'something feels off, I can't say why' — lets someone else help you test it instead of carrying the discarded accuracy alone. The instrument was never unreliable; discarding its readings was the actual cost."
  },
  "58": {
    "wiring": "You push people towards better, and the ones who stayed will tell you plainly that their lives improved in measurable ways because of it. You spot the fixable flaw in an arrangement instantly and have the energy to actually change it. Your vitality is contagious in company.",
    "root": "A moment felt finished early on only once nothing was left to improve, so rest got tied to a finish line that kept moving because it was never really about completion. That equation is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether an ordinary, unimproved moment could feel complete on its own.",
    "defaultMode": "You improve people without ever being asked, correcting a partner's method, their timing, their choice of words, and calling the whole thing help. Nothing ever arrives complete for you, so a genuinely good evening still ends with the note about what would have made it better.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see that the improving never stops because you've tied your own rest to a finish line that was never actually the point of the moment. Others notice the note about what could have been better before you'd admit you'd added it. What feels like standards to you sometimes reads to them as an inability to let a good moment be enough.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine delight in making things better is a rare relational asset most people experience as pressure rather than pleasure. Declaring one ordinary evening finished on purpose, before your instinct to improve it activates, tests whether the moment was actually lacking or just met with a habit. The vitality was never the problem; knowing when to stop applying it is."
  },
  "59": {
    "wiring": "You tell people the truth at close range, and that's the reason your important relationships have a depth other people's quietly lack. You dissolve the distance between yourself and another person quickly, walking straight through the small talk into what's actually going on for them. You ask the direct question and stay fully present for the answer.",
    "root": "Your real appetites felt early on like they'd drive people away if fully seen, so opening up just enough to secure someone — rather than to genuinely be known by them — became the working strategy. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether the full version was actually as costly as it felt.",
    "defaultMode": "You soften what you actually want, agree to the shape of the relationship that keeps the peace, and slowly become somebody your partner doesn't fully know. You use closeness itself as a strategy, opening up just enough to secure someone rather than to genuinely be seen by them.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how much of yourself you've quietly kept unmentioned in a relationship that looks intimate from the outside. Others sense the managed distance before you'd admit it's there. What feels like being appropriately private to you sometimes reads to them as never quite fully known.",
    "underusedStrength": "The capacity to dissolve distance and get to what's real quickly is a genuine relational advantage most people never develop. Naming one specific thing you usually withhold, out loud, in the relationship that matters most, converts your general skill at intimacy into something actually pointed at yourself. The skill was never the problem; using it on your own concealment is."
  },
  "60": {
    "wiring": "You work with what is actually here in front of you, and that's made you the person others come to when a plan first meets reality. You accept a constraint quickly, spending your energy on what remains possible. Your realism openly protects the people closest to you.",
    "root": "Hope felt early on like a setup, and disappointment felt like the real world simply arriving on schedule, so expecting less became a form of protection rather than an accurate read of what was actually possible. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether wanting more was actually as dangerous as it felt.",
    "defaultMode": "You name the obstacle first, every single time, and the people around you hear a no before they've even finished describing what they hoped for. You keep rules that made sense during an older, harder period of your life and enforce them on a present that no longer requires them.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when your realism has curdled into a reflex that closes down possibility before actually evaluating it. Others notice they've stopped bringing you their hopes before you'd admit you'd become the reason. What feels like protecting them to you sometimes reads to them as being shut down preemptively.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine realism — an accurate sense of what a situation actually requires — is a rare relational asset that protects people from wasted hope. Asking what would have to be true for a hope to work, instead of listing why it won't, keeps the skill constructive instead of purely defensive. The instinct for constraints was never wrong; aimed only at rejection, it stops being useful."
  },
  "61": {
    "wiring": "You receive insight that other people have to go looking for, and your closest relationships have been reshaped more than once by something you simply knew. You bring genuine wonder into a partnership, asking out loud the questions most couples abandon in their twenties. You hold that insight lightly enough to check it honestly against another person's view.",
    "root": "An idea felt worth sharing only once it was total early on, so a partner questioning an unformed thought felt like an argument about reality rather than a comment on something still forming. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether sharing the unfinished version was actually as costly as it felt.",
    "defaultMode": "You build an inner certainty that nobody is permitted to inspect, and when a partner questions it you hear ordinary disagreement as proof they're not deep enough for you. Isolation follows quickly, and the ideas grow stranger without anyone left to test them against.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when your private certainty has stopped being insight and started being isolation dressed up as depth. Others notice being shut out of the process before you'd admit you'd shut them out. What feels like protecting the idea to you sometimes reads to them as never being trusted enough to be told what you believe.",
    "underusedStrength": "Insight that arrives whole and unbidden is a genuine relational gift most people never experience this directly. Sharing the unformed version of a thought deliberately, before it feels total, lets someone actually be inside the process with you instead of only receiving the finished conclusion. The depth was never the problem; the timing of when you let it be seen was."
  },
  "62": {
    "wiring": "You untangle knots into language, and the people you love have watched their own confusion become manageable inside a single conversation with you. You name a feeling accurately enough that the other person recognises it immediately. Your precision is a form of care rather than pedantry.",
    "root": "If you described a thing precisely enough, early on, you never had to actually be inside it emotionally, so precision became a way to manage feeling from a safe distance rather than a neutral communication tool. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether being inside the feeling was actually as unbearable as it seemed.",
    "defaultMode": "You win the exchange on accuracy and lose the person, correcting one small detail while they were telling you something that frightened them badly. You explain your feelings rather than have them, offering a well-organised account of your own distress that leaves your partner nothing at all to comfort.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see when your precision has become a way to stay outside a feeling rather than actually communicate it. Others notice they're getting an account of your distress rather than access to it before you'd admit the difference. What feels like being clear to you sometimes reads to them as being kept at a careful distance.",
    "underusedStrength": "Genuine precision in naming a feeling is a rare relational skill that changes how someone is able to carry what they're going through. Letting one feeling stay messy and unedited, without finding the exact word for it first, gives your partner something to actually hold rather than something to admire from a distance. The skill was never wrong; using it to stay outside the feeling instead of inside it was."
  },
  "63": {
    "wiring": "You question what everyone else quietly accepts, and the people closest to you have been saved real trouble by that habit more than once. You find the flaw in a proposal fast, early enough that fixing it is still cheap. Your inquiry is genuinely curious rather than hostile when you're at your best.",
    "root": "Committing without proof felt early on like exactly how people get caught out, so nothing got fully committed to — including affection — until it had survived enough scrutiny to feel safe. That caution is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether trust without full proof was actually survivable.",
    "defaultMode": "Doubt eventually turns inward and starts quietly auditing the very people who love you most, one reassurance at a time. You interrogate a partner's reassurance, testing it over and over until they run out of ways to prove something that was never genuinely in question. You dismantle your own good news within a day of receiving it.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how many good, genuine reassurances have quietly expired under your scrutiny before you'd let yourself simply receive them. Others notice the enthusiasm draining from a room before you'd admit your scrutiny was the cause. What feels like diligence to you sometimes reads to them as an inability to actually accept anything good.",
    "underusedStrength": "The instinct to test claims rigorously before accepting them is a genuine relational asset when aimed at proposals and plans rather than at the people who love you. Setting a firm limit on how much reassurance-testing a relationship gets before you're required to simply receive it keeps the skill useful instead of exhausting everyone near you. The scrutiny was never wrong; unlimited, it stopped being scrutiny and became avoidance."
  },
  "64": {
    "wiring": "You picture possibilities in a volume that most people never once experience, and that abundance has made you genuinely interesting to live alongside. You hold several futures at the same time without collapsing them into one too early. You see the way out of someone's situation at the exact moment they see only a wall.",
    "root": "Clarity felt early on like it had to be reached alone before you were fit for company, so a genuinely unresolved picture became something to hide rather than something to bring into a relationship while it was still forming. That belief is old and still runs underneath your closest relationships. It formed before you'd tested whether sharing the unfinished picture was actually welcome.",
    "defaultMode": "Memories, options, and half-formed images cycle for days, and you go quiet in the middle of a relationship because there's nothing yet you're able to say cleanly. Others read that silence as absence and start quietly making their own plans around you. You also replay old scenes on a loop, rewriting conversations from years ago.",
    "blindSpot": "You cannot easily see how often your silence during a confusing stretch is read as absence rather than as the internal process it actually is. Others notice they've started planning around you before you'd think to explain what was happening internally. What feels like private processing to you sometimes reads to them as being missing exactly when they needed you.",
    "underusedStrength": "Holding several futures in mind at once, without forcing premature clarity, is a genuinely rare relational gift most people never develop. Naming the confusion out loud while it's happening — 'I'm somewhere unclear right now, stay with me' — keeps the relationship intact through the very stretches that used to isolate you both. The imagination was never the problem; keeping the process silent is what cost you."
  }
});
